[thought] Okay, the user wants me to list 25 distinct things that on-device AI can do that a cloud model can't. And I need to number them and give two full sentences each. Let me think.

First, I remember that on-device AI processes data locally, which means it doesn't need to send data to the cloud. So, things like real-time processing and local decision-making come to mind. For example, a phone can use AI to recognize a face in real time without internet, which a cloud model can't do because it needs data from the cloud.

Another thing is local data processing. The phone can handle data on the device, which is faster and more secure. So, features like image recognition without internet might be a point. Also, local processing of user data, like in a camera app, which a cloud model can't do because it would need to send data to the cloud.

Then there's the aspect of power efficiency. On-device AI can save battery by processing data locally, which a cloud model can't do because it's offloaded to the cloud. Also, the ability to use AI without internet, like a phone using its own camera for image recognition, which is a key point.

